perturbations.a:
Two similar planets, each with a weight of {eq}10^{24} kg {/eq}, revolve around the midpoint between the two planets. If the distance separating the two planets is {eq}2 \times 10^8 m {/eq} (as measured from their centers), how quickly is each of the planets moving in their orbit? (EXPLAIN STEP BY STEP)

Orbital Velocity

Orbital velocity is defined as the velocity required by a celestial body to orbit another massive body. If the mass of the massive body is M and orbital radius is r then orbital velocity is given by

{eq}\begin{align} v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} \end{align} {/eq}

Let us recap essential information from the question

• Weight of each planet {eq}m = 10^{24} \ kg {/eq}
• Distance separating two planet {eq}d = 2 \times 10^8 \ m {/eq}

Here we will assume that both the planet are orbiting the center of mass of the two particle system and whole mass is concentrated at the center of mass i.e. mid point as both the particle are similar. Now total mass on the center of mass

• Weight {eq}M = m+ m = 2m = 2\times 10^{24} \ kg {/eq}
• Distance of each planet from midpoint i.e. orbital radius {eq}r = d/2 = 10^8 \ m {/eq}

Now orbital velocity is given by

{eq}\begin{align} v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} \end{align} {/eq}

{eq}\begin{align} v & = \sqrt{\frac{6.67 \times 10^{-11} \ N.m^2.kg^{-2} \times 2 \times 10^{24} \ kg }{10^{8} \ m}} \\ & = 1154.99 \ m/s \end{align} {/eq}

{eq}\begin{align} v & \approx 1155 \ m/s \end{align} {/eq}

